<<~ ahu #/core-claim>>

! Disk Projection — Seed and Projection Surfaces

Every ''`@bag`'' maps to one Automerge document — one CRDT, one coordinate axis of the recipe query plan (see residency-model). ''The operator's mind originates; the disk carrier and the CRDT record-set both //project// that origin, each in its native grain'' (the co-projection model, operator ruling 2026-06-11): disk projects thought as one whole markdown meme — the way the operator writes it; the CRDT doc projects the same thought as tid-sized records — the way TW5 merges, addresses, and syncs it; the VM decomposes for transclusion (#/granularity). Co-equal in dignity, single in merge seat: ''merge authority routes through the CRDT alone'', never file-vs-record reconciliation — anything else opens the dual-write trap (Tonsky crdt-filesync; Kleppmann).

The node vessel SHALL write an editable, re-readable ''Artifact'' of a bag's CRDT state to disk (the "disk projection"), and re-ingest operator edits. This rides ''two on-disk surfaces'', split by bag //role// — ''source vs output'':

| Surface | Role | Bags | Source of record | Format |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| ''`bags/`'' | ''seed / canon'' — definitions, rules, templates, protocol memes | `lares`, `lararium`, `sdm` (corpus) | disk-leaning (authored seed; round-trips through the store) | whole-carrier `.md` (see #granularity) |
| ''`wikis/{slug}/`'' | ''projection / output'' — instantiated playspace content | `@<wikiSlug>` (per-wiki content) | store-leaning (the merge seat; disk projects the rendered artifact) | memetic-wikitext `.md` |

The distinction reads ''definition vs instance''. An FTLS Power Card //definition// (its rules, its template) lives in `bags/sdm/`; a character's //filled-in// card and their sheet project to `wikis/{slug}/`. `bags/` seeds the world; `wikis/` carries a running instance's output. More generally, `wikis/` reads as the ''live working surface'' — saved-but-unpromoted content, fed by the typed ''`@working`'' write layer (saved tiddlers, never drafts) — and `bags/` reads as ''canon'', reached only by a cap-gated promotion `MOVE`; definition-vs-instance names one shape of this ''working↔canon shore'' (`wiki-layer-ontology#shore-law`).

⚠ ''Read the `disk-leaning` grading as GRAIN, never as a write door.'' The word carries two grounds: the ''authoring grain'' — a hand edits one whole carrier, one meme, one git history (#granularity) — which holds under the designed model; and the alpha-era ''surviving copy'', where disk outlasts a paved substrate and so answers first. The second retires, and until it does the tree's instruments side with it. Full reading, the three roles `bags/` carries, and the ordering the transition owes: <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology#/alpha-transition">> (operator, 2026-09-13).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/bag-roles>>

!! Bag roles — which bags project, which ride the mesh

Disk projection serves ''content'' bags (definitions + instances). ''State / view / runtime'' bags carry no `BagMirrorConfig` and so never write to disk — they ride the CRDT sync mesh.

| Bag | Role | Disk surface |
|---|---|---|
| `lares` / `lararium` / `sdm` | seed / canon (definitions; `lararium` = the engine's ''memetic corpus'' — see #oracle-split) | `bags/` |
| `@<wikiSlug>` | wiki content (instance) | `wikis/{slug}/` |
| `@oracle` | ''runtime system island'' — genesis-loaded engine BLOBs + bag→doc oracle + genesis-cid | ''none'' — genesis-loaded + federated read-only; regenerable (see #oracle-split) |
| `@personal` | operator view-state, wiki-instance-bound | ''none'' — mesh-synced (see #personal) |
| `@working` | per-wiki ''saved'' working layer (saved tiddlers, pre-promotion) | `wikis/{slug}/` — live, tracked |
| `@draft` | per-wiki ''unsaved'' drafts (TW5 draft state) | ''none'' — runtime |
| `@temp` | volatile session layer | ''none'' — runtime |
| `@daemon` | operator's sovereign authority doc | ''none'' — private; syncs to operator devices via the admin-doc surface |

The daemon wiki's working layer projects under `wikis/daemon/`, while the daemon control bag projects nowhere.

The projector enforces this structurally: ''a bag absent from the mirror list never writes to disk.'' A tiddler MAY also opt out per-record with `disk-projection: no`. The opt-out holds in both directions: an address whose records never project (a receipt fact, a verb summons, a ward audit; `neverProjects`) and a carrier that declares `disk-projection: no` never ingest, so a file standing there refuses by name (`never-projected-path`), creates nothing, and leaves the wave's other carriers to land.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/oracle-split>>

!! @oracle vs @lararium — the shared floor and the corpus

| Kind | Name | What it holds | Source | Disk | Scope |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| ''memetic corpus'' | ''`lararium`'' | authored engine self-doc memes | `bags/` seed | tracked | corpus |
| ''oracle floor'' | ''`@oracle`'' | the system rows and blob descriptors named by the genesis seed | seed-named projection of the live oracle doc; the shared frozen bake and its no-writer rule stay owed | none | every vessel |

The public read-face projects only seed-named rows and blob descriptors, without Automerge history. Each snapshot carries the seed CID beside its own CID. The Kahu Plane road owes a frozen floor address derived from the seed CID and a no-writer rule.

The floor does not carry per-vessel registry pointers. A registry names documents at one scope, and that scope's owner writes it. `lares` and `lararium` remain system bag names and recipe members; their per-vessel document pointers still ride `@oracle`; bootstrap/catalog relocation remains owed. The read-face carries no oracle document itself.

The bulb carries the exact seed bytes by seed CID at `/bulb/<cid>.bin`; the public oracle read-face projects the seed-named floor rows. LAW THE BUILD OWES: one porch answer per Nexus names the floor CID; that Nexus's Kahu steward signs it, and a herm carries and presents it without signing it. No tender name reaches a stranger; the steward seal carries the authority. The Kahu Plane owns the steward-signing shape; R-WIRE owes answer carriage.

''Why `@oracle` carries no disk mirror.'' The public snapshot selects its row names from the genesis seed and reads their values from the live oracle doc; the frozen seed-derived floor and its no-writer rule stay owed. Compiled engine blobs derive from the build and ride the content-addressed store. The CAS carries bytes by CID, while the read-face carries only the seed-named rows and descriptors.

''Why `lararium` stays a seed bag.'' The engine self-docs carry authored prose, and git tracks that corpus.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/personal>>

!! @personal — wiki-instance-bound, off-disk

`@personal` holds the operator's view-state for a specific wiki-instance (`$:/StoryList`, `$:/state/folded/*`, `$:/palette`), keyed per ''(PersonaGroup × recipe-fingerprint)'' (see personal-slot). It holds NO canon (no `bags/` seed) and NO wiki content (no `wikis/` instance projection) — it carries a ''per-operator overlay''.

It carries ''no disk mirror.'' Rationale:

* ''Disk stays per-device; @personal crosses devices.'' It follows the operator across their devices through the admin-doc / Keyhive CRDT mesh, not through a disk artifact. Projecting it to disk would conflate the two and invite divergence.
* ''It still appears in the projection.'' A `wikis/{slug}/` projection renders from the live, recipe-resolved view — and the recipe composes `@personal`'s view-state into that view. So the operator's ordering and folds //show up// in the projected wiki without `@personal` itself ever touching disk.

A hand MAY add a future debug-only disk projection behind an explicit flag; it MUST NOT become the default.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/projection-law>>

!! Projection law

# ''RENDER, not copy'' (Fontany-Fuller-Zelenka). Disk projection recomposes the whole carrier from normalized tiddler records via the shore's recompose inverse (`expandMemeRefs`, published as `$tw.lares.meme.recompose`, running inside the island VM). It never copies stored bytes raw — byte-fidelity comes from the proven parse∘render fixed point, not retention.
# ''Store → disk runs one direction; ingest runs another.'' The projector runs one way (store → disk): no disk byte ever reaches a record through it. It does READ disk — the Confluence gate below must know what the operator's hands left there before it may write over it — and ''a read that decides whether to write performs no ingest''. Re-reading operator edits INTO the records (disk → store) stays a ''separate file-watcher / ingest path''. The `writing` guard set prevents the projector's own writes from echoing back through ingest.
# ''The git diff carries the operator's signature.'' When a residency ''MOVE'' (the canon ACTION verb) relocates a tiddler between bags, the disk side effect lands as a file move between surfaces; the diff records the operator's intent. ''Promotion'' rides this rite — a cap-gated `MOVE` from the `@working` live layer to canon crosses the ''working↔canon shore'', and a `MOVE` of a bag's pointer between planes (`@catalog ⇄ @oracle`) crosses the ''plane shore''. Both stay loose causal-island crossings — no shared-now, no synchronous bridge (`wiki-layer-ontology#shore-law`).
# ''Reconcile per carrier root.'' A child change climbs `$fragment-parent` to its root; the group flushes as ONE carrier write (carrier-whole at rest). The gate holds no timer: every change marks its root, each reconcile reads the settled wiki state, a root marked while its reconcile runs reconciles once more, and no storm of marks starves a root. A mount scan marks every root whose `$origin-bag` names a mirror, in the turn that subscribes the wiki's change bus; each reconcile then honors the pack-member and per-record projection guards. The projection-side gate skips byte-identical writes entirely — no event, no mtime churn. The gate's mechanics: `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/node/disk-projector#/the-servo`.
# ''BOTH LEGS RECONCILE, AND BOTH RUN WIRED'' (operator ruling, 2026-09-13: //"Approve conflict-surfacing in BOTH legs of round-trip memes."//). The projecting leg carries its own pure three-way decision (`projection-gate`, `decideProjection`) over the SAME three streams the ingest leg reads — the records' render · the base (the last agreement) · the bytes standing on disk. Its verdicts mirror `ingest-gate`'s: ''noop'' (`disk-matches-records`, the echo gate riding first · `canonical-equivalent` · `records-unmoved`) · ''project'' (`absent-on-disk` · `disk-unmoved` · `records-taken` · `restore-answered`) · ''pending-removal'' (`vanished`) · ''conflict'' (`no-base` · `both-moved`). The gate decides; it never writes. `LarDiskProjector.flush` consults it on every flush the byte-identity skip does not already answer, and owns every I/O and every alert: ''project'' writes, ''noop'' stands down silently, ''pending-removal'' and ''conflict'' stand down LOUDLY on the ward-alert rail — ONE rail, `wardKind` naming the mechanism that actually stood down, never a second idiom. Ingest's fourth verdict, `refuse`, rides ABSENT here: it grades a parse of untrusted disk bytes, and the render this leg writes comes from the VM registry, whose faults surface as a null `CarrierFile` before the gate consults anything.<br>''A PROJECTION OF AN UNCHANGED CARRIER WRITES NOTHING.'' Disk and records co-project one carrier and neither stands as canon, so a file whose bytes say what the records render stands unchanged, whatever framing those bytes wear. The gate's `≈` clause transposes ingest's rule 3 (`render(parse(disk))` == the records' render) and answers ''noop'' `canonical-equivalent`, ahead of every clause that reads the base: a first seed holds no base, and a fresh adoption that rewrote every file standing in another framing would dirty a person's working tree with edits no person made. Only a real change writes, and that write lands the canonical render, so the framing normalizes as the change lands.<br>''NO BASE AND A REAL DIFFERENCE: NEITHER LEG PICKS.'' Where no base stands for a carrier and its file says something else than the records render, nothing names which side moved last: a hand may have edited the file while the vessel rested. The gate answers ''conflict'' `no-base`. The projector writes nothing and surfaces one keyed standoff on the alert rail (#/write-ward). The equivalence clause rides ahead, so a first seed of an unchanged corpus pins its bases, writes no file and raises no alert, and a path that holds no file and no base takes its first projection (`absent-on-disk`). A person settles a standoff one of three ways: ingest the file (`lares ingest`), which lands the disk's words in the records; save in the wiki until both sides say the same thing; or take the records' side under the standoff's key (`lares shore take-records`), which writes the records' render over the file and pins the base (`records-taken`). A standoff's key folds the bag, the carrier, the path, the two states and the base they stand against, so an answer under a key reaches the one state its giver read: where any of those moved since, it takes nothing, and the standoff that stands keeps its own key. `lares shore` lists every standoff the active wiki's island holds; the verb rides `wiki-act` into that island, where a node-held door (`claimShoreVerb`, `shore-verb.ts`) answers from the projector the island mounts. A projector handed no tree holds its bases in memory for its own life.<br>''A BASE STANDS AT ONE PATH.'' Each base names the content file its agreement stood at (`path`), beside the two hashes. A base reached at one path names no base at another: a carrier that a siting rule or a filetype change moved meets its new path as a first projection, and a file already standing there reads through the no-base clauses. The projector and the ingest scan both ask the tree for the base AT a path (`baseAt`), under one spelling (`syncedPath`). ''A carrier holds one base per path.'' The projector pins a base at the mirror's file and the ingest gesture pins one at the source it read; the two stand side by side and neither displaces the other. So an ingest from a holding directory beside the mirror leaves the mirror's base standing: the mirror's file stood unmoved, only the records moved, and the projector writes the records' render there (`disk-unmoved`); the holding file's next scan reads its own base. Where a hand also edited the mirror's file, both sides moved past the mirror's base and the carrier stands off as `both-moved`. Before a carrier's own files write, surface or pend, the projector reads the tree afresh, so a base a sibling writer pinned answers first.<br>''A VANISHED FILE STANDS AS A PENDING REMOVAL'' (house reading; whether a pending removal may ever settle with no person stays the operator's: `stable-founding-road#/open`). A content file absent at the very path its base names may mean a delete, a branch switch, a checkout or a slip, and the bytes cannot say which. The gate answers ''pending-removal'' `vanished`, whatever the records say since: the projector writes nothing back, nothing tombstones the records, and the base stays. One alert per bag names how many carriers stand pending and a few of them; the projector raises it when its reconcile queue drains, by that queue's own idle handshake, so a branch switch reads as one alert with one count, and the alert clears when the set empties. A person answers through `lares shore`: `restore` writes the records' render back and pins the base (`restore-answered`); `remove` deletes the carrier root in the wiki, so the wiki shore's own router lands the tombstone in the working layer, where it shadows canon (`outbound-bridge#/the-shore-law`), and removes the slot children. `remove` leaves the base in the tree: a carrier that returns from beneath its tombstone (a lowered kāpae, a write the store refused) reads as pending again and asks its person afresh. An answer names each carrier it reaches, or `all` with the count its giver read; over more than one carrier the count rides too, and a count that moved refuses (`pending-count-moved`). That count replaces the fraction brake: it names how many, and a set whose members changed under an unchanged count reads the same to it. A file that returns by any hand clears its pending removal with no answer, and `lares shore` looks at the disk before it lists: at every carrier that stands off or pends, and at every carrier whose base names a mirror path where no file stands (`vanished`), so a file a hand removed with no watcher running pends by the time the list answers. A path that never held a base keeps `absent-on-disk`: a first projection writes the file. A content file whose declared `.meta` left reads as a disk state of its own (the body alone, no canonical view), so the base clauses decide it and no body gives way on the strength of a missing sidecar. A sidecar standing with no body beside it reads the same way wherever no base names a body there: a pointer whose bytes the content tier lacks projects its sidecar alone, that sidecar meets the gate as an empty body beside its bytes, and a hand's edit to it stands off like any other. `restore` writes a vanished carrier's body and its sidecar back together.<br>''THE WATCHER TOMBSTONES NOTHING BY ITSELF.'' `lares watch` holds no grace clock and no deletion fraction. It keeps each vanished address in memory, hands it to the vessel's shore (`shore sense`), which reconciles those carriers and raises the pending-removal alert, and tells the shore again when a path returns. It sends a deletion only as a rename's source, paired by content: one vanished address and one ARRIVED file (a file at an address that held no base) that share a hash, each unique in the wave, and the source leaves the watcher's vanished set only when the island's answer names its re-link (`pairedDeletions`, `confirmedRenames`; the one-shot scan's `resolveRenames` holds the same uniqueness), so no pair the island's delete gate would read as a tombstone ever leaves a gesture.<br>''AN UNLINK READS THE FILE FIRST.'' The projector removes a file only where it can prove the file holds nothing a hand added: its bytes equal the disk side of the base that stands at that path, or they say what the records render, byte for byte or through the equivalence (`judgeHome`). One judge serves every unlink: the earlier path of a carrier a siting rule moved, a sibling left at an earlier extension, a mirror the carrier left, and the file of a carrier the records dropped (there the base alone can vouch). A file that fails the proof stays, and the carrier stands off under one key that names the paths; nothing writes at the new path until the earlier one settles. A person settles it as any standoff: ingest the earlier file, or take the records' side under the key, which removes that file and lets the write land. The earlier path comes from two memories: the projector's own siting for the session, and the base's `path`, which names it across a restart. The base's `path` names an earlier home only where the disk ward would let that mirror write (#/write-ward: inside the mirror's own root, or directly in the bags root under the widened grant): the ingest gesture pins a base at whatever source it read, and a source a person keeps in a holding directory beside the mirror meets no judge and stays whole. A file under a mirror root that a bag still holding the carrier shares stays whole. A sidecar standing alone leaves only where the base names exactly that state (a pending pointer's own sidecar); any other lone sidecar stays.<br>''THE BASE HOLDS TWO SIDES.'' An agreement names what the disk held and what the records rendered (`synced-tree`, a `{disk, records}` pair per carrier and path). The two hashes read equal where the disk holds the canonical render, which every projector write leaves, and differ where the disk stands in another framing. On an equivalence the projector pins the base at that agreement and writes no byte; the ingest gesture pins the same pair, taking the records side from the island's row (`canonicalHash`), or through the pure door below where a wave's rows stayed behind the outcome's cap. The ingest scan reads the disk side, so the file reads `unchanged`. "The disk stands where the base left it" reads the disk side and "the records stand where the base left them" reads the records side, in both gates, so a later hand edit to such a file ingests cleanly and a later record edit projects cleanly; read against one hash alone, each would surface as a conflict nobody caused. A row of the tree in any other shape names no base, and the tree says once how many such rows it met.<br>''THE TREE RESTS ONE FILE PER ROW.'' The tree's directory (`synced-tree/` under the vessel's projection directory) holds one file per base, named by its carrier and its path, and each write lands one row whole: a temp file in that directory, then a rename. A writer touches the rows it changed and reads no other row to do so. The projectors of every island and the ingest gesture share the tree from separate workers and processes, and two that write in one moment drop no row; the tree holds no lock and reads no clock. ''AN OBSERVATION RESTS THE MOMENT IT GETS RECORDED'': a set lands its row and a removal unlinks its row before either returns, and no writer holds a change back for a later write. Two writers of one row therefore leave the later observation standing, whole, and a row one writer removed never returns from another writer's memory. A landing the disk refuses gets named, and the writer takes back the row it held before the act: the directory's word stands, nothing retries on a timer, and the next gate that weighs the carrier finds its base behind and pins anew. ''A BASE LEAVES WITH ITS OWN FILE'': where the records drop a carrier, or a confirmed rename moves it, the bases at the files that left go with them, and a base at a holding copy that still stands stays, so that copy's next scan reads `unchanged` and no later ingest of its directory lands the carrier again. A vessel founded under the single-file tree keeps that file (`synced-tree.json`) beside the directory: nothing reads it, the tree names it once at each open, and every carrier reads as never agreed, so an unchanged corpus pins anew and writes nothing while a real difference stands off for a person.<br>''The view comes from ONE door:'' `canonicalizeCarrierText` (`@lararium/tw5/carrier-canonical`, a PURE subpath — no barrel, no wasm), injected at the composition root beside `carrierFileFn`. It reuses `memeticIngestOps`, so exactly one implementation of `≈` stands in the tree, and it carries the ahu-fidelity guard — which BITES HARDER on this shore, because here an equivalence pins a base, and a base pinned over a lossy round trip would let a later write replace the operator's bytes with a text that no longer carries the slot. A lossy round trip therefore answers null, and an ABSENT canonical view never reads as equivalence.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/granularity>>

!! Granularity — carrier-whole at rest (operator ruling, 2026-06-11)

''Each stratum carries its own natural grain; grain MUST NOT leak between strata:''

| Stratum | Grain | Why |
|---|---|---|
| ''disk'' (hands, git, editors) | ''whole memetic-wikitext carriers'' — one meme, one `.md` | the human edits a document; one meme, one git history |
| ''CRDT doc'' (database) | record-grain ("tid-sized") | merge granularity, per-ahu fields, addressable `#fragment` children, partial sync |
| ''live wiki VM'' | decomposed — parent + ahu children as tiddlers | transclusion, per-slot templates, kahea |

''A meme resides on disk as one whole carrier.'' Decomposition lives only between the shore (`memeticWikitextDeserializer`) and the VM; every path back to disk MUST route through the recompose inverse (`expandMemeRefs` / `exportMemeText`). ''Fragment files MUST NOT appear on any surface a human edits'' — a `#ahu`-grain file on disk constitutes a grain leak (a degraded projection), and the round-trip law holds byte-faithful: anything in the operator's source survives.

//Retired to legacy-design-time (the prior intent here — decompose `bags/` into one `.tid` file per tiddler — burned 2026-06-11):// tid-sized files serve the database, never the operator's hands; managing meme-sized thought as tid-swarms makes the exact TW5-community friction that birthed memetic-wikitext. The kupono vectors live in `tests/e2e/carrier-roundtrip.test.ts`.

`wikis/{slug}/` stays memetic-wikitext `.md` on the same law.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/projection-routing>>

!! Projection routing — tiddler-groups + the standard TW5 path (operator ruling, 2026-06-11)

Write-back routes by ''type'', in three rules:

# ''Group rule.'' `type: text/memetic-wikitext` records form a ''tiddler-group'' keyed by the carrier parent (a fragment URI belongs to its parent's group). A group MUST write back as ''ONE markdown-meme `.md`'' — the whole carrier (#/granularity). A child's change re-flushes its group, never its own file.
# ''Default rule.'' Every other tiddler follows the ''standard TW5 path'' — `$tw.utils.generateTiddlerFileInfo` semantics, run inside the island's own TW5 VM: `.tid` for tw5-wikitext, content-file + `.meta` sidecar for foreign types, `.json` for unsafe fields. One tiddler, one file, TW5's own format law — for a record whose title sites (a `lar:///w.w.w/…` loci name; `carrierBaseRelPath` returns null for any other title). A foreign-titled record (a `.json` / `.multids` / `.tid` pack member) owns no file of its own: its membership rides `$:/config/OriginalTiddlerPaths` and its pack re-renders whole by REPACK (wiki-layer-ontology#/pack-refinements).
# ''Cascade rule.'' Siting and format overrides ride TW5's OWN cascade tiddlers — `$:/config/FileSystemPaths` and `$:/config/FileSystemExtensions`, first non-empty filter result wins — read EXACTLY as the stock filesystem adaptor reads them (a REAL tiddler in the wiki, `tiddlerExists`; `tw5-file-info.ts` `readSitingCascades`). The render shore (`exportCarrierFile`) hands the projector a ruled `relPath` when a rule reached the title; the projector sites there, remembers the siting per mirror (the adaptor's `$tw.boot.files` memory — a delete runs after the tiddler has gone), unlinks the previous files when a rule moves a carrier and those files stand agreed (#/projection-law, an unlink reads the file first), and otherwise sites by the loci law below. ONE spelling: a stock server and an island read the same tiddler and site the same file; a `lar:` twin honoured by one door only would make the disagreement. A rule that escapes the mirror meets the disk ward. Parity against the fork's own `generateTiddlerFileInfo`: `packages/lararium-tw5/tests/tw5-file-info-parity.test.ts`; the projector's half: `packages/lararium-node/tests/ruled-siting.test.ts`.

''The reading leg routes by declaration.'' On ingest a carrier's `.meta` sidecar names its family first: a declared type no carrier reader admits sends the body to TW5's own registry, whatever the body shows. With no such declaration a LIVE head decides (a SOH sigil standing outside every fence); a head shown inside a fence opens nothing. So a document that teaches the frame keeps its filetype and every sidecar field on the way in, and leaves under the extension it arrived with (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/action-handler.ts`, `readsAsMemetic`; `packages/lararium-tw5/tests/carrier-family-by-declaration.test.ts`).

''One siting fn.'' `carrierBaseRelPath` (bag-paths.ts) sites every carrier extension-less by its lar-URI and returns null for a fragment (`#slot`), an unstable root, or a non-lar URI — so a fragment owns no disk file by construction (the grain-leak guard, #granularity). `meme-write.ts` recomposes the whole carrier through `expandMemeRefs`; no per-node template renders a record to its own file. The kupono vectors live in `tests/e2e/carrier-roundtrip.test.ts` (V3/V4).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/write-ward>>

!! The disk ward — sovereign-island write confinement (operator ruling, 2026-06-11)

Cascade tiddlers compose through the ''recipe'' — a library bag can overlay `$:/config/FileSystemPaths` — so every path reaching the projector counts as ''untrusted input''. Policy lives in the cascade; the ''mechanism'' holds at the write choke-point (`confineMirrorWrite`, bag-paths), regardless of what the cascade emitted:

# ''Default:'' a mirror's writes confine to its OWN bag subdir (`bags/{bagname}/…`). Absolute paths and any traversal resolving outside the root refuse.
# ''Widened grant'' (`allowBagsRootFiles` — a capability riding the manifest's `diskMirrors`, never a cascade-settable flag): the mirror MAY place files ''DIRECTLY in the root-bags-dir'' — one level up, the dir holding every `@{bagname}` subdir. The rule reads `dirname(candidate) === bagsDir` exactly, so the grant stays //structurally incapable// of escaping `bags` or entering another bag's subdir — no new subdirs, no depth, no siblings.
# ''Refusals surface LOUDLY'' (`[disk-ward] write refused …` with the reason) — a silent skip would hide a probing overlay from the operator.

''The alert chain (operator ruling, 2026-06-11):'' every ward trip raises a `$:/tags/Alert` in the operator's eye, routed through the admin VM:

```
projector onRefusal (wiki island)
  → IslandMsg_Event "disk-ward:refused" (payload.verb = "ward-alert")
  → main's generic worker.event → adminVm.placeVerb bridge
  → admin VM "ward-alert" reactor:
      (a) DURABLE audit record  lar:///ha.ka.ba/bags/daemon/ledger/ward/<id>
          — lands in the daemon wiki's working layer, stamped `disk-projection: no`
      (b) reads its own active-wiki marker → the operator's PINNED VM
  → admin:wiki-alert (kind "disk-ward") → main → system-alert verb
  → pinned island writes $:/temp/lares/alert/disk-ward tagged $:/tags/Alert
      — ring-0 operator surface (@temp, island-local, self-clearing on reboot)
```

Visibility holds by construction: the durable record lands in the daemon wiki's working layer and never projects to disk; the live alert lives in the pinned wiki's `@temp` (the operator's own island; never federated — `noise`/`data` stay node-local). The reactor carries no cap-gate: the signal originates from the island's own mechanism, grants nothing, and writes only audit + alert.

''What the gate says over the real corpus.'' `packages/lararium-node/tests/seed-fixed-point-corpus.test.ts` walks the seed's own gesture over every tracked file of both house bags (the scan, the INGEST reactor behind the outcome's row cap, the settling gesture, the projector with the island's congruences) and reads the scratch tree back: no file's bytes move, no file arrives or leaves, no standoff surfaces, the gesture and the projector pin the same base, and a second scan reads every carrier `unchanged`. A projector that then meets the same corpus with an empty tree (a vessel that lost its bases) moves no file, raises no standoff and pins every base again at the same path under the same two hashes. A carrier whose render differs from its bytes stands in a named framing class (the meta block's layout, a blank margin, the re-stamped check, a sidecar's layout); the vector prints the count per class and fails on a carrier outside every class.

''One rail, three kinds.'' A pending-removal set rides the same rail under `wardKind: pending-removal`, keyed by the bag and its sorted members: one audit row and one alert per set, cleared when the set ends or its membership moves. ''One rail, two refusals.'' A projection conflict calls the projector's `onConflict`, which sends `ward-alert` with `wardKind: projection-conflict` down the same alert chain a disk-ward refusal rides, so the operator reads it in the same pinned-wiki alert and the same durable ledger. No second rail — a signal with no audience repeats the fault of a fold that stands ready and uncalled. The projector leaves the disk file untouched, and each reconcile that meets the standoff may send the alert again. ''A standoff surfaces once, under its own key.'' The projector folds the bag, the carrier and the two hashes (the disk's and the records') into a standoff key. It raises `onConflict` on ENTERING a standoff and stays quiet at every later reconcile of the same states; a standoff whose states moved reads as a new one, and the one it left clears. A carrier that stops projecting (`disk-projection: no`, a bag with no mirror, a carrier gone) leaves its standoff the same way. The rail files ONE audit row for a key (`…/ledger/ward/<key>`) and places the live alert at `$:/temp/lares/alert/disk-ward/<key>`, so two standoffs show two alerts. The reconcile that finds the standoff gone raises `onConflictResolved`, the rail carries the key with `resolved`, and the wiki removes that alert; a resolution files no row. A disk-ward refusal names no standoff: it keeps one row per refusal and the kind's one coalescing alert.

Ward vectors: `packages/lararium-node/tests/disk-confinement.test.ts` (traversal, absolute, cross-bag, above-bags, new-subdir, grantless, inert-grant, refusal-signal) · `packages/lararium-node/tests/projection-conflict-surfaces.test.ts` (the projection verdicts, canonical equivalence and the corpus conflict controls) · `packages/lararium-node/tests/projection-standoff-alert.test.ts` and `packages/lararium-tw5/tests/ward-alert-standoff.test.ts` (one surfacing, one row and one alert per standoff; the clear) · `packages/lararium-node/tests/projection-conflict-reaches-the-operator.test.ts` · `packages/lararium-node/tests/shore-no-base-stands-off.test.ts` (a hand edit made while the vessel rested survives a boot that holds no base; the three settlements) · `packages/lararium-node/tests/shore-verb.test.ts` (the answer door) · `packages/lararium-node/tests/shore-vanished-file-pends.test.ts` (a deleted file stays gone and unwritten, one alert names a mass vanish, each answer) · `packages/lararium-node/tests/shore-pending-answers.test.ts` (a lost sidecar, a re-sited path, the count handshake) · `packages/lares-cli/tests/watch-vanish-pairs.test.ts` (the watcher's one decision, and no clock) · `packages/lararium-node/tests/shore-unlink-reads-first.test.ts` (a hand edit at a path the carrier left stays, at each of the four unlinks).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cli>>

!! CLI capability

The operator drives the disk-projection Artifact through the `@lares/cli` surface (alongside `lares wiki` / `lares bag` / `lares act`). The live projector runs inside the vessel (TW5 change → level-triggered reconcile → render → write); the CLI exposes the deliberate snapshot/inspect verbs. `namedBagMirror(bagId, scope, mirrorRoot)` configures a bag mirror; the island reconstructs it from the serializable `diskMirrors` manifest field.

